1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,973
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,153 --> 00:00:04,673
Living paycheck to paycheck is exhausting.

3
00:00:04,853 --> 00:00:06,908
Counting days until payday.

4
00:00:07,088 --> 00:00:09,619
Checking your balance before buying groceries.

5
00:00:09,799 --> 00:00:12,670
Feeling your stomach drop when an unexpected

6
00:00:12,670 --> 00:00:13,583
bill shows up.

7
00:00:13,763 --> 00:00:15,307
And it's incredibly common.

8
00:00:15,487 --> 00:00:18,159
But here's what almost nobody talks about:

9
00:00:18,159 --> 00:00:20,958
what actually changes when you finally break

10
00:00:20,958 --> 00:00:21,594
out of it.

11
00:00:21,774 --> 00:00:23,422
Because it's a lot more than just

12
00:00:23,422 --> 00:00:24,921
having some money in the bank.

13
00:00:25,271 --> 00:00:27,766
Paycheck to paycheck means that when the

14
00:00:27,766 --> 00:00:29,139
next paycheck arrives,

15
00:00:29,139 --> 00:00:30,948
the last one is already gone.

16
00:00:31,128 --> 00:00:32,022
No buffer.

17
00:00:32,202 --> 00:00:33,189
No cushion.

18
00:00:33,369 --> 00:00:34,913
Every dollar spoken for.

19
00:00:35,263 --> 00:00:38,027
Surveys by groups like LendingClub and PYMNTS

20
00:00:38,027 --> 00:00:40,484
have repeatedly found that a majority of

21
00:00:40,484 --> 00:00:43,248
Americans say they live paycheck to paycheck,

22
00:00:43,248 --> 00:00:45,828
including many people earning six figures.

23
00:00:46,008 --> 00:00:48,016
It's not only a low income problem.

24
00:00:48,196 --> 00:00:49,253
It's a gap problem.

25
00:00:49,433 --> 00:00:51,890
When spending rises to meet income,

26
00:00:51,890 --> 00:00:53,995
any income can feel too small.

27
00:00:54,346 --> 00:00:56,728
Today, twenty five things that change when

28
00:00:56,728 --> 00:00:58,827
you stop living paycheck to paycheck.

29
00:00:59,007 --> 00:01:00,122
Some are about money.

30
00:01:00,302 --> 00:01:01,855
Some are about your health,

31
00:01:01,855 --> 00:01:04,214
your relationships and the way you think.

32
00:01:04,394 --> 00:01:06,122
And we'll look at the whole thing

33
00:01:06,122 --> 00:01:07,064
from three angles.

34
00:01:07,244 --> 00:01:09,547
The numbers, meaning what living on the

35
00:01:09,547 --> 00:01:10,727
edge actually costs.

36
00:01:10,907 --> 00:01:13,144
The psychology, meaning what it does to

37
00:01:13,144 --> 00:01:13,775
your brain.

38
00:01:13,955 --> 00:01:14,921
And the playbook,

39
00:01:14,921 --> 00:01:16,683
meaning exactly how to get out.

40
00:01:17,034 --> 00:01:19,422
If you're living paycheck to paycheck right

41
00:01:19,422 --> 00:01:21,145
now, this video is a picture of

42
00:01:21,145 --> 00:01:22,978
what's waiting on the other side.

43
00:01:23,158 --> 00:01:24,950
And if you've already escaped,

44
00:01:24,950 --> 00:01:26,922
it's a reminder of why it's worth

45
00:01:26,922 --> 00:01:27,639
staying out.

46
00:01:27,819 --> 00:01:29,340
Let's start with the sources.

47
00:01:29,691 --> 00:01:31,978
Our numbers come from named sources.

48
00:01:32,158 --> 00:01:34,989
Household savings data comes from the Federal

49
00:01:34,989 --> 00:01:37,695
Reserve's survey on the economic well being

50
00:01:37,695 --> 00:01:38,009
of U.

51
00:01:38,189 --> 00:01:38,537
S.

52
00:01:38,717 --> 00:01:39,623
households.

53
00:01:39,803 --> 00:01:42,940
Payday loan and overdraft information comes from

54
00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:45,620
the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau.

55
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:48,423
Credit score information comes from FICO.

56
00:01:48,603 --> 00:01:50,740
And the research on how money stress

57
00:01:50,740 --> 00:01:54,183
affects thinking comes from economist Sendhil Mullainathan

58
00:01:54,183 --> 00:01:55,964
and psychologist Eldar Shafir.

59
00:01:56,144 --> 00:01:58,082
This video is education,

60
00:01:58,082 --> 00:01:59,778
not financial advice.

61
00:02:00,128 --> 00:02:00,813
Angle one.

62
00:02:00,993 --> 00:02:01,701
The numbers.

63
00:02:01,881 --> 00:02:04,354
Living paycheck to paycheck isn't just stressful.

64
00:02:04,534 --> 00:02:05,462
It's expensive.

65
00:02:05,642 --> 00:02:07,430
Being broke costs money.

66
00:02:07,610 --> 00:02:08,330
Here's how.

67
00:02:08,681 --> 00:02:11,731
The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau has reported

68
00:02:11,731 --> 00:02:14,379
that overdraft fees have typically been around

69
00:02:14,379 --> 00:02:15,530
thirty five dollars.

70
00:02:15,710 --> 00:02:17,699
And a typical payday loan fee of

71
00:02:17,699 --> 00:02:20,559
fifteen dollars per one hundred borrowed works

72
00:02:20,559 --> 00:02:22,486
out to an annual rate of almost

73
00:02:22,486 --> 00:02:23,791
four hundred percent.

74
00:02:24,141 --> 00:02:25,511
Think about what that means.

75
00:02:25,691 --> 00:02:28,377
A twenty dollar purchase that overdraws your

76
00:02:28,377 --> 00:02:30,636
account can end up costing fifty five

77
00:02:30,636 --> 00:02:31,124
dollars.

78
00:02:31,304 --> 00:02:33,592
A three hundred dollar payday loan for

79
00:02:33,592 --> 00:02:35,820
two weeks can cost forty five dollars

80
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:37,446
in fees, and if you roll it

81
00:02:37,446 --> 00:02:38,409
over, much more.

82
00:02:38,589 --> 00:02:40,912
These costs fall hardest on people with

83
00:02:40,912 --> 00:02:41,805
the least room.

84
00:02:41,985 --> 00:02:44,115
That's the cruel math of living on

85
00:02:44,115 --> 00:02:44,679
the edge.

86
00:02:45,029 --> 00:02:46,515
Then there's the credit card.

87
00:02:46,695 --> 00:02:48,183
When there's no cushion,

88
00:02:48,183 --> 00:02:50,043
every surprise goes on a card,

89
00:02:50,043 --> 00:02:52,833
often at interest rates above twenty percent,

90
00:02:52,833 --> 00:02:54,880
according to the Federal Reserve.

91
00:02:55,060 --> 00:02:57,239
A one thousand dollar emergency,

92
00:02:57,239 --> 00:02:59,827
paid off slowly with minimum payments,

93
00:02:59,827 --> 00:03:02,551
can cost hundreds of dollars in interest

94
00:03:02,551 --> 00:03:04,185
and take years to clear.

95
00:03:04,365 --> 00:03:07,271
Meanwhile, the next surprise is already on

96
00:03:07,271 --> 00:03:07,825
its way.

97
00:03:08,175 --> 00:03:09,974
And there are hidden costs too.

98
00:03:10,154 --> 00:03:11,849
Late fees on bills.

99
00:03:12,029 --> 00:03:14,725
Higher insurance premiums when you can only

100
00:03:14,725 --> 00:03:16,104
afford to pay monthly.

101
00:03:16,284 --> 00:03:18,975
Buying small packages at higher unit prices

102
00:03:18,975 --> 00:03:21,416
because you can't afford the bulk size.

103
00:03:21,596 --> 00:03:22,954
Reconnection fees.

104
00:03:23,134 --> 00:03:24,493
Each one is small.

105
00:03:24,673 --> 00:03:27,077
Together, they can add up to hundreds

106
00:03:27,077 --> 00:03:29,417
or even thousands of dollars a year,

107
00:03:29,417 --> 00:03:31,302
a tax on not having a buffer.

108
00:03:31,652 --> 00:03:34,296
The Federal Reserve found that sixty three

109
00:03:34,296 --> 00:03:36,815
percent of adults would cover a surprise

110
00:03:36,815 --> 00:03:39,333
four hundred dollar expense with cash or

111
00:03:39,333 --> 00:03:40,278
its equivalent.

112
00:03:40,458 --> 00:03:42,603
Which means more than a third would

113
00:03:42,603 --> 00:03:43,523
have to borrow,

114
00:03:43,523 --> 00:03:45,913
sell something, or couldn't cover it at

115
00:03:45,913 --> 00:03:46,158
all.

116
00:03:46,509 --> 00:03:48,275
Here's the good news hidden in all

117
00:03:48,275 --> 00:03:48,691
of that.

118
00:03:48,871 --> 00:03:51,007
Because being broke is so expensive,

119
00:03:51,007 --> 00:03:53,558
getting even slightly ahead saves you money

120
00:03:53,558 --> 00:03:54,270
immediately.

121
00:03:54,450 --> 00:03:56,283
The first one thousand dollars you keep

122
00:03:56,283 --> 00:03:57,364
doesn't just sit there.

123
00:03:57,544 --> 00:03:58,776
It stops the fees,

124
00:03:58,776 --> 00:04:00,899
the interest and the penalties.

125
00:04:01,079 --> 00:04:02,292
In a real sense,

126
00:04:02,292 --> 00:04:04,793
a small emergency fund can earn a

127
00:04:04,793 --> 00:04:07,900
better return than almost any investment,

128
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:10,704
because of all the costs it prevents.

129
00:04:11,054 --> 00:04:13,456
So, twenty five things that change when

130
00:04:13,456 --> 00:04:14,687
you break the cycle.

131
00:04:14,867 --> 00:04:17,073
The first few show up within weeks.

132
00:04:17,424 --> 00:04:18,561
Change number one.

133
00:04:18,741 --> 00:04:21,258
You stop checking your balance before buying

134
00:04:21,258 --> 00:04:21,830
groceries.

135
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:24,385
This is the first thing many people

136
00:04:24,385 --> 00:04:24,827
notice.

137
00:04:25,007 --> 00:04:26,971
The little calculation in your head at

138
00:04:26,971 --> 00:04:28,211
the checkout disappears.

139
00:04:28,391 --> 00:04:30,005
You stop putting items back.

140
00:04:30,185 --> 00:04:32,205
You stop praying the card goes through.

141
00:04:32,385 --> 00:04:33,359
It sounds small,

142
00:04:33,359 --> 00:04:35,550
but it's a daily stress that quietly

143
00:04:35,550 --> 00:04:36,707
drains your energy,

144
00:04:36,707 --> 00:04:37,863
and when it's gone,

145
00:04:37,863 --> 00:04:38,898
you feel lighter.

146
00:04:39,248 --> 00:04:41,687
People who've made this shift often describe

147
00:04:41,687 --> 00:04:43,682
it as the first moment they realized

148
00:04:43,682 --> 00:04:44,902
something had changed.

149
00:04:45,082 --> 00:04:46,301
Not a big purchase.

150
00:04:46,481 --> 00:04:47,653
Not a celebration.

151
00:04:47,833 --> 00:04:50,366
Just a normal Tuesday at the store,

152
00:04:50,366 --> 00:04:51,235
and no fear.

153
00:04:51,415 --> 00:04:53,374
That's what a cushion feels like in

154
00:04:53,374 --> 00:04:53,934
real life.

155
00:04:54,285 --> 00:04:55,457
Change number two.

156
00:04:55,637 --> 00:04:57,762
Overdraft fees disappear.

157
00:04:58,112 --> 00:05:00,056
When there's always a buffer in your

158
00:05:00,056 --> 00:05:02,163
checking account, you stop overdrawing.

159
00:05:02,343 --> 00:05:04,319
Every overdraft fee you don't pay is

160
00:05:04,319 --> 00:05:05,745
money back in your pocket.

161
00:05:05,925 --> 00:05:08,159
For people who used to overdraw a

162
00:05:08,159 --> 00:05:09,378
few times a month,

163
00:05:09,378 --> 00:05:11,883
that can mean over a thousand dollars

164
00:05:11,883 --> 00:05:12,763
a year saved.

165
00:05:13,114 --> 00:05:15,286
Try this: keep a minimum buffer in

166
00:05:15,286 --> 00:05:17,907
checking, like one hundred or two hundred

167
00:05:17,907 --> 00:05:19,952
dollars, that you treat as zero.

168
00:05:20,132 --> 00:05:22,255
Set a low balance alert just above

169
00:05:22,255 --> 00:05:22,442
it.

170
00:05:22,622 --> 00:05:24,308
And if your bank allows it,

171
00:05:24,308 --> 00:05:26,617
turn off overdraft coverage entirely,

172
00:05:26,617 --> 00:05:28,990
so a purchase gets declined instead of

173
00:05:28,990 --> 00:05:30,738
costing thirty five dollars.

174
00:05:31,088 --> 00:05:32,353
Change number three.

175
00:05:32,533 --> 00:05:33,833
Late fees vanish.

176
00:05:34,184 --> 00:05:35,648
When money is tight,

177
00:05:35,648 --> 00:05:37,990
bills get paid in the order they

178
00:05:37,990 --> 00:05:38,502
scream.

179
00:05:38,682 --> 00:05:39,843
Some end up late.

180
00:05:40,023 --> 00:05:41,902
Late fees on credit cards,

181
00:05:41,902 --> 00:05:44,793
utilities, rent and phone bills can each

182
00:05:44,793 --> 00:05:47,394
cost twenty five to forty dollars or

183
00:05:47,394 --> 00:05:47,756
more.

184
00:05:47,936 --> 00:05:48,823
With a cushion,

185
00:05:48,823 --> 00:05:50,656
you can pay everything on time,

186
00:05:50,656 --> 00:05:52,963
regardless of when your paycheck lands.

187
00:05:53,313 --> 00:05:54,329
Once you're ahead,

188
00:05:54,329 --> 00:05:55,797
put every bill on autopay.

189
00:05:55,977 --> 00:05:57,736
Because the money is there,

190
00:05:57,736 --> 00:06:00,276
autopay stops being risky and becomes a

191
00:06:00,276 --> 00:06:00,993
time saver.

192
00:06:01,173 --> 00:06:03,129
You don't have to remember due dates

193
00:06:03,129 --> 00:06:03,564
anymore.

194
00:06:03,744 --> 00:06:05,439
The system just runs.

195
00:06:05,790 --> 00:06:07,148
Change number four.

196
00:06:07,328 --> 00:06:09,432
You never need a payday loan or

197
00:06:09,432 --> 00:06:10,857
a cash advance again.

198
00:06:11,208 --> 00:06:13,812
Payday loans are designed for people with

199
00:06:13,812 --> 00:06:14,447
no buffer.

200
00:06:14,627 --> 00:06:15,817
The fees are high,

201
00:06:15,817 --> 00:06:18,991
and the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau has

202
00:06:18,991 --> 00:06:21,768
found that many borrowers roll their loans

203
00:06:21,768 --> 00:06:23,157
over again and again,

204
00:06:23,157 --> 00:06:24,611
paying fees each time.

205
00:06:24,791 --> 00:06:27,668
A small emergency fund closes that trap

206
00:06:27,668 --> 00:06:28,553
permanently.

207
00:06:28,903 --> 00:06:31,543
If you're currently stuck in payday loans,

208
00:06:31,543 --> 00:06:33,114
the way out is to build a

209
00:06:33,114 --> 00:06:35,251
small fund while paying them down,

210
00:06:35,251 --> 00:06:36,948
even a few hundred dollars.

211
00:06:37,128 --> 00:06:39,907
Some credit unions also offer small,

212
00:06:39,907 --> 00:06:42,608
lower cost loans as an alternative.

213
00:06:42,788 --> 00:06:44,786
Every step away from those fees is

214
00:06:44,786 --> 00:06:45,667
money you keep.

215
00:06:46,018 --> 00:06:47,271
Change number five.

216
00:06:47,451 --> 00:06:49,402
Your credit score starts to climb.

217
00:06:49,752 --> 00:06:50,960
According to FICO,

218
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:53,646
payment history is the biggest factor in

219
00:06:53,646 --> 00:06:54,519
a FICO score,

220
00:06:54,519 --> 00:06:56,265
about thirty five percent.

221
00:06:56,445 --> 00:06:58,438
When you pay every bill on time

222
00:06:58,438 --> 00:07:00,561
and stop maxing out credit cards,

223
00:07:00,561 --> 00:07:02,168
your score tends to rise.

224
00:07:02,348 --> 00:07:05,053
Lower credit card balances relative to your

225
00:07:05,053 --> 00:07:06,122
limits also help.

226
00:07:06,472 --> 00:07:09,545
A higher score means lower interest rates

227
00:07:09,545 --> 00:07:10,520
on car loans,

228
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:12,544
mortgages and credit cards.

229
00:07:12,724 --> 00:07:13,875
On a large mortgage,

230
00:07:13,875 --> 00:07:15,602
a better rate can save tens of

231
00:07:15,602 --> 00:07:17,732
thousands of dollars over the life of

232
00:07:17,732 --> 00:07:18,250
the loan.

233
00:07:18,430 --> 00:07:20,732
So getting off the paycheck to paycheck

234
00:07:20,732 --> 00:07:22,679
treadmill doesn't just save fees.

235
00:07:22,859 --> 00:07:24,926
It makes every future loan cheaper.

236
00:07:25,276 --> 00:07:26,750
Change number six.

237
00:07:26,930 --> 00:07:28,184
You sleep better.

238
00:07:28,534 --> 00:07:32,409
The American Psychological Association's Stress in America

239
00:07:32,409 --> 00:07:35,282
surveys have consistently found money to be

240
00:07:35,282 --> 00:07:37,487
one of the most common sources of

241
00:07:37,487 --> 00:07:38,890
stress for Americans.

242
00:07:39,070 --> 00:07:41,136
Money worries follow people to bed.

243
00:07:41,316 --> 00:07:43,093
They lie awake running numbers.

244
00:07:43,273 --> 00:07:44,276
With a cushion,

245
00:07:44,276 --> 00:07:46,082
that late night math stops.

246
00:07:46,433 --> 00:07:49,007
People who build their first emergency fund

247
00:07:49,007 --> 00:07:51,762
often say the biggest change wasn't financial.

248
00:07:51,942 --> 00:07:52,731
It was sleep.

249
00:07:52,911 --> 00:07:54,877
It was not feeling anxious every time

250
00:07:54,877 --> 00:07:55,674
the phone rang.

251
00:07:55,854 --> 00:07:57,579
You can't put a price on that,

252
00:07:57,579 --> 00:07:59,362
but it may be the most valuable

253
00:07:59,362 --> 00:08:00,800
thing on this whole list.

254
00:08:01,150 --> 00:08:02,578
Change number seven.

255
00:08:02,758 --> 00:08:04,174
You fight less about money.

256
00:08:04,524 --> 00:08:06,474
Money is one of the most common

257
00:08:06,474 --> 00:08:08,488
sources of conflict for couples,

258
00:08:08,488 --> 00:08:11,257
and stress makes every disagreement sharper.

259
00:08:11,437 --> 00:08:13,157
When every dollar is tight,

260
00:08:13,157 --> 00:08:15,512
every purchase becomes a negotiation.

261
00:08:15,692 --> 00:08:16,635
With a buffer,

262
00:08:16,635 --> 00:08:20,069
small spending decisions stop feeling like threats.

263
00:08:20,420 --> 00:08:22,725
Try this: once you have some breathing

264
00:08:22,725 --> 00:08:24,726
room, set up a monthly money date

265
00:08:24,726 --> 00:08:25,818
with your partner.

266
00:08:25,998 --> 00:08:26,973
Twenty minutes.

267
00:08:27,153 --> 00:08:28,994
Look at the numbers together,

268
00:08:28,994 --> 00:08:31,596
celebrate progress, and agree on the next

269
00:08:31,596 --> 00:08:31,914
goal.

270
00:08:32,094 --> 00:08:34,559
Couples who talk about money regularly tend

271
00:08:34,559 --> 00:08:35,878
to fight about it less.

272
00:08:36,229 --> 00:08:37,471
Change number eight.

273
00:08:37,651 --> 00:08:40,004
Payday stops being the most important day

274
00:08:40,004 --> 00:08:40,751
of the month.

275
00:08:41,101 --> 00:08:43,121
When you live paycheck to paycheck,

276
00:08:43,121 --> 00:08:45,373
your whole life revolves around payday.

277
00:08:45,553 --> 00:08:47,028
Bills get timed around it.

278
00:08:47,208 --> 00:08:48,810
Plans get timed around it.

279
00:08:48,990 --> 00:08:50,768
Once you're a full month ahead,

280
00:08:50,768 --> 00:08:51,973
payday is just a day.

281
00:08:52,153 --> 00:08:53,804
Your paycheck refills the tank,

282
00:08:53,804 --> 00:08:55,613
but you're never running on empty.

283
00:08:55,964 --> 00:08:57,966
Many people aim to get one full

284
00:08:57,966 --> 00:09:00,550
month ahead, so this month's spending is

285
00:09:00,550 --> 00:09:02,488
paid from last month's income.

286
00:09:02,668 --> 00:09:04,759
It's one of the most freeing setups

287
00:09:04,759 --> 00:09:05,954
in personal finance.

288
00:09:06,134 --> 00:09:08,129
Start by building a buffer of a

289
00:09:08,129 --> 00:09:09,416
few hundred dollars,

290
00:09:09,416 --> 00:09:11,283
and grow it until it equals a

291
00:09:11,283 --> 00:09:12,763
full month of spending.

292
00:09:13,114 --> 00:09:14,518
Change number nine.

293
00:09:14,698 --> 00:09:16,855
You start paying lower prices for the

294
00:09:16,855 --> 00:09:17,554
same things.

295
00:09:17,905 --> 00:09:18,749
With a buffer,

296
00:09:18,749 --> 00:09:20,678
you can buy the big pack instead

297
00:09:20,678 --> 00:09:21,702
of the small one,

298
00:09:21,702 --> 00:09:23,270
stock up when prices drop,

299
00:09:23,270 --> 00:09:25,078
and wait for a sale instead of

300
00:09:25,078 --> 00:09:27,309
buying the moment something runs out.

301
00:09:27,489 --> 00:09:30,202
Unit prices on bigger packages are often

302
00:09:30,202 --> 00:09:31,355
noticeably lower.

303
00:09:31,705 --> 00:09:33,911
This works for more than groceries.

304
00:09:34,091 --> 00:09:36,186
You can buy a reliable used car

305
00:09:36,186 --> 00:09:38,754
with cash instead of financing a worse

306
00:09:38,754 --> 00:09:39,025
one.

307
00:09:39,205 --> 00:09:41,428
You can replace a broken appliance with

308
00:09:41,428 --> 00:09:42,226
a durable one,

309
00:09:42,226 --> 00:09:44,905
instead of the cheapest option available today.

310
00:09:45,085 --> 00:09:47,767
Having money lets you make decisions based

311
00:09:47,767 --> 00:09:49,427
on value, not desperation.

312
00:09:49,778 --> 00:09:51,032
Change number ten.

313
00:09:51,212 --> 00:09:52,965
Your insurance can get cheaper.

314
00:09:53,315 --> 00:09:55,572
Many insurers charge less if you pay

315
00:09:55,572 --> 00:09:57,390
your premium in full upfront,

316
00:09:57,390 --> 00:09:58,644
rather than monthly.

317
00:09:58,824 --> 00:10:00,605
And with an emergency fund,

318
00:10:00,605 --> 00:10:03,047
you can choose a higher deductible on

319
00:10:03,047 --> 00:10:04,498
car or home insurance,

320
00:10:04,498 --> 00:10:06,742
which usually lowers your premium.

321
00:10:06,922 --> 00:10:08,841
You're taking on a bit more risk

322
00:10:08,841 --> 00:10:11,241
yourself, because now you can afford to.

323
00:10:11,591 --> 00:10:13,858
Try this: ask your insurer how much

324
00:10:13,858 --> 00:10:16,189
you'd save by paying annually and by

325
00:10:16,189 --> 00:10:17,744
raising your deductible.

326
00:10:17,924 --> 00:10:20,489
Make sure your emergency fund can comfortably

327
00:10:20,489 --> 00:10:21,686
cover the deductible.

328
00:10:21,866 --> 00:10:22,894
For many people,

329
00:10:22,894 --> 00:10:25,724
these two changes together save a meaningful

330
00:10:25,724 --> 00:10:26,881
amount every year.

331
00:10:27,231 --> 00:10:28,635
Change number eleven.

332
00:10:28,815 --> 00:10:31,565
A car breakdown becomes an annoyance,

333
00:10:31,565 --> 00:10:32,531
not a crisis.

334
00:10:32,881 --> 00:10:34,955
When you live paycheck to paycheck,

335
00:10:34,955 --> 00:10:37,030
a six hundred dollar car repair can

336
00:10:37,030 --> 00:10:38,512
set off a chain reaction.

337
00:10:38,692 --> 00:10:39,944
You can't get to work,

338
00:10:39,944 --> 00:10:41,595
you put the repair on a card,

339
00:10:41,595 --> 00:10:43,417
you fall behind on another bill.

340
00:10:43,597 --> 00:10:44,579
With a cushion,

341
00:10:44,579 --> 00:10:45,954
you pay the mechanic,

342
00:10:45,954 --> 00:10:47,002
refill the fund,

343
00:10:47,002 --> 00:10:47,788
and move on.

344
00:10:48,138 --> 00:10:50,648
This is exactly what an emergency fund

345
00:10:50,648 --> 00:10:51,110
is for.

346
00:10:51,290 --> 00:10:52,857
It doesn't prevent bad luck.

347
00:10:53,037 --> 00:10:55,160
It stops bad luck from turning into

348
00:10:55,160 --> 00:10:55,464
debt.

349
00:10:55,644 --> 00:10:58,343
Every surprise you handle with cash instead

350
00:10:58,343 --> 00:11:00,415
of credit is a small victory that

351
00:11:00,415 --> 00:11:02,110
keeps your progress intact.

352
00:11:02,461 --> 00:11:03,726
Change number twelve.

353
00:11:03,906 --> 00:11:04,835
You can say no.

354
00:11:05,185 --> 00:11:07,065
No to a boss who treats you

355
00:11:07,065 --> 00:11:07,483
badly.

356
00:11:07,663 --> 00:11:09,916
No to a high pressure sales pitch.

357
00:11:10,096 --> 00:11:11,970
No to a friend's loan request you

358
00:11:11,970 --> 00:11:12,708
can't afford.

359
00:11:12,888 --> 00:11:14,577
When you have nothing saved,

360
00:11:14,577 --> 00:11:16,568
you often can't afford to say no.

361
00:11:16,748 --> 00:11:18,763
Savings give you the power to walk

362
00:11:18,763 --> 00:11:19,059
away.

363
00:11:19,409 --> 00:11:21,858
Some people call this a freedom fund.

364
00:11:22,038 --> 00:11:24,276
Even a few months of expenses saved

365
00:11:24,276 --> 00:11:26,130
can change how you show up at

366
00:11:26,130 --> 00:11:26,450
work.

367
00:11:26,630 --> 00:11:28,729
You can negotiate more confidently,

368
00:11:28,729 --> 00:11:31,727
because you're not terrified of losing everything.

369
00:11:31,907 --> 00:11:34,261
That confidence alone can lead to better

370
00:11:34,261 --> 00:11:34,496
pay.

371
00:11:34,846 --> 00:11:36,401
Change number thirteen.

372
00:11:36,581 --> 00:11:38,324
You can take a chance on something

373
00:11:38,324 --> 00:11:38,683
better.

374
00:11:39,033 --> 00:11:40,925
A better job in another city.

375
00:11:41,105 --> 00:11:43,348
A training course that pays off next

376
00:11:43,348 --> 00:11:43,659
year.

377
00:11:43,839 --> 00:11:45,932
A new role with a short gap

378
00:11:45,932 --> 00:11:46,939
between jobs.

379
00:11:47,119 --> 00:11:50,799
Opportunities often require a little money upfront,

380
00:11:50,799 --> 00:11:53,180
or a short period without income.

381
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:55,682
Without savings, people stay stuck.

382
00:11:55,862 --> 00:11:57,522
With them, they can take the leap.

383
00:11:57,872 --> 00:12:00,387
Think of your savings as an opportunity

384
00:12:00,387 --> 00:12:02,516
fund, not just an emergency fund.

385
00:12:02,696 --> 00:12:04,861
Some of the biggest income jumps people

386
00:12:04,861 --> 00:12:07,248
make happen because they had enough cushion

387
00:12:07,248 --> 00:12:09,192
to take a risk that others couldn't

388
00:12:09,192 --> 00:12:09,580
afford.

389
00:12:09,931 --> 00:12:11,533
Change number fourteen.

390
00:12:11,713 --> 00:12:14,697
Credit cards stop being your emergency fund.

391
00:12:15,047 --> 00:12:17,088
When a card is your only backup,

392
00:12:17,088 --> 00:12:19,830
every emergency adds debt at high interest.

393
00:12:20,010 --> 00:12:21,346
Once you have savings,

394
00:12:21,346 --> 00:12:23,412
you can use a card for convenience

395
00:12:23,412 --> 00:12:25,295
and rewards, and pay it in full

396
00:12:25,295 --> 00:12:26,024
every month.

397
00:12:26,204 --> 00:12:27,922
The card goes from a trap to

398
00:12:27,922 --> 00:12:28,352
a tool.

399
00:12:28,702 --> 00:12:31,318
If you're carrying credit card debt right

400
00:12:31,318 --> 00:12:33,041
now, the path is to build a

401
00:12:33,041 --> 00:12:34,317
small cushion first,

402
00:12:34,317 --> 00:12:36,168
then attack the card balance,

403
00:12:36,168 --> 00:12:37,955
highest interest rate first.

404
00:12:38,135 --> 00:12:39,839
When the balance hits zero,

405
00:12:39,839 --> 00:12:42,049
keep paying it in full every month,

406
00:12:42,049 --> 00:12:44,195
and never let it carry over again.

407
00:12:44,545 --> 00:12:45,996
Change number fifteen.

408
00:12:46,176 --> 00:12:48,397
Interest starts working for you instead of

409
00:12:48,397 --> 00:12:49,032
against you.

410
00:12:49,382 --> 00:12:51,935
When you live paycheck to paycheck,

411
00:12:51,935 --> 00:12:53,176
you pay interest:

412
00:12:53,176 --> 00:12:54,562
on cards, on loans,

413
00:12:54,562 --> 00:12:55,802
on cash advances.

414
00:12:55,982 --> 00:12:57,279
Once you have savings,

415
00:12:57,279 --> 00:12:59,047
you can earn interest instead.

416
00:12:59,227 --> 00:13:01,417
A high yield savings account at an

417
00:13:01,417 --> 00:13:03,865
online bank or credit union often pays

418
00:13:03,865 --> 00:13:06,699
far more than a traditional savings account,

419
00:13:06,699 --> 00:13:08,631
though rates change over time.

420
00:13:08,981 --> 00:13:10,548
It's a complete reversal.

421
00:13:10,728 --> 00:13:13,035
Instead of paying twenty percent to a

422
00:13:13,035 --> 00:13:14,282
credit card company,

423
00:13:14,282 --> 00:13:16,465
you're earning a few percent from a

424
00:13:16,465 --> 00:13:16,777
bank.

425
00:13:16,957 --> 00:13:19,301
On a ten thousand dollar emergency fund,

426
00:13:19,301 --> 00:13:21,353
that can mean a few hundred dollars

427
00:13:21,353 --> 00:13:21,763
a year.

428
00:13:21,943 --> 00:13:23,060
Not life changing,

429
00:13:23,060 --> 00:13:25,046
but it's the first time money is

430
00:13:25,046 --> 00:13:25,728
paying you.

431
00:13:26,078 --> 00:13:27,065
Quick pause.

432
00:13:27,245 --> 00:13:29,664
If you're living paycheck to paycheck right

433
00:13:29,664 --> 00:13:31,071
now, or you just got out,

434
00:13:31,071 --> 00:13:32,759
I'd love to hear your story in

435
00:13:32,759 --> 00:13:33,491
the comments.

436
00:13:33,671 --> 00:13:35,501
And if this video is helping,

437
00:13:35,501 --> 00:13:36,132
subscribe.

438
00:13:36,312 --> 00:13:38,418
I make videos like this every couple

439
00:13:38,418 --> 00:13:40,525
of days, and subscribing is the best

440
00:13:40,525 --> 00:13:42,221
way not to miss the next one.

441
00:13:42,401 --> 00:13:44,782
Now, change number sixteen.

442
00:13:45,132 --> 00:13:46,804
Change number sixteen.

443
00:13:46,984 --> 00:13:48,830
You can finally start investing.

444
00:13:49,180 --> 00:13:51,605
Investing money you might need next month

445
00:13:51,605 --> 00:13:53,972
is risky, because markets can fall right

446
00:13:53,972 --> 00:13:55,333
when you need the cash.

447
00:13:55,513 --> 00:13:57,495
But once you have a real emergency

448
00:13:57,495 --> 00:13:59,420
fund, you can invest for the long

449
00:13:59,420 --> 00:14:00,761
term without that fear.

450
00:14:00,941 --> 00:14:03,843
That's when wealth building really begins.

451
00:14:04,193 --> 00:14:06,065
At a seven percent illustration,

452
00:14:06,065 --> 00:14:08,465
investing two hundred dollars a month for

453
00:14:08,465 --> 00:14:10,689
thirty years could grow to roughly two

454
00:14:10,689 --> 00:14:12,796
hundred forty four thousand dollars.

455
00:14:12,976 --> 00:14:15,342
That's what becomes possible when you're no

456
00:14:15,342 --> 00:14:16,772
longer living on the edge.

457
00:14:16,952 --> 00:14:18,775
The cushion is the foundation.

458
00:14:18,955 --> 00:14:21,219
Investing is what you build on top.

459
00:14:21,569 --> 00:14:23,171
Change number seventeen.

460
00:14:23,351 --> 00:14:25,557
You can grab the full employer match.

461
00:14:25,907 --> 00:14:28,408
Many people living paycheck to paycheck skip

462
00:14:28,408 --> 00:14:30,227
their workplace retirement plan,

463
00:14:30,227 --> 00:14:32,160
or contribute less than the match,

464
00:14:32,160 --> 00:14:34,150
because they need every dollar now.

465
00:14:34,330 --> 00:14:35,782
Once you have breathing room,

466
00:14:35,782 --> 00:14:37,685
you can contribute enough to get every

467
00:14:37,685 --> 00:14:38,486
matching dollar.

468
00:14:38,666 --> 00:14:40,593
That's an instant return on your money.

469
00:14:40,944 --> 00:14:43,303
A common formula is fifty cents for

470
00:14:43,303 --> 00:14:45,191
every dollar you contribute,

471
00:14:45,191 --> 00:14:47,213
up to six percent of your pay.

472
00:14:47,393 --> 00:14:49,483
On a fifty thousand dollar salary,

473
00:14:49,483 --> 00:14:51,942
that's fifteen hundred dollars a year of

474
00:14:51,942 --> 00:14:53,848
extra money from your employer.

475
00:14:54,028 --> 00:14:55,688
Missing it for years is one of

476
00:14:55,688 --> 00:14:57,791
the hidden costs of living paycheck to

477
00:14:57,791 --> 00:14:58,289
paycheck.

478
00:14:58,639 --> 00:15:00,369
Change number eighteen.

479
00:15:00,549 --> 00:15:02,604
You start making better decisions.

480
00:15:02,954 --> 00:15:04,423
In their book Scarcity,

481
00:15:04,423 --> 00:15:07,617
Sendhil Mullainathan and Eldar Shafir describe how

482
00:15:07,617 --> 00:15:10,236
not having enough narrows your focus onto

483
00:15:10,236 --> 00:15:12,472
the urgent problem in front of you,

484
00:15:12,472 --> 00:15:15,475
and leaves less mental bandwidth for everything

485
00:15:15,475 --> 00:15:15,794
else.

486
00:15:15,974 --> 00:15:17,720
In one study they discuss,

487
00:15:17,720 --> 00:15:20,740
simply thinking about a big financial problem

488
00:15:20,740 --> 00:15:23,962
lowered people's performance on reasoning tests.

489
00:15:24,312 --> 00:15:26,221
This means that getting out of paycheck

490
00:15:26,221 --> 00:15:28,081
to paycheck doesn't just give you more

491
00:15:28,081 --> 00:15:28,375
money.

492
00:15:28,555 --> 00:15:30,482
It gives you back mental space.

493
00:15:30,662 --> 00:15:33,077
People make better long term choices when

494
00:15:33,077 --> 00:15:35,492
they're not constantly putting out fires.

495
00:15:35,672 --> 00:15:37,577
It's one of the most powerful and

496
00:15:37,577 --> 00:15:39,771
least discussed benefits on this list.

497
00:15:40,121 --> 00:15:41,502
Change number nineteen.

498
00:15:41,682 --> 00:15:44,058
You start planning months ahead instead of

499
00:15:44,058 --> 00:15:44,341
days.

500
00:15:44,691 --> 00:15:45,749
When money is tight,

501
00:15:45,749 --> 00:15:47,918
your planning horizon shrinks to the next

502
00:15:47,918 --> 00:15:48,394
paycheck.

503
00:15:48,574 --> 00:15:49,973
Once you have a buffer,

504
00:15:49,973 --> 00:15:51,980
you can think about next quarter,

505
00:15:51,980 --> 00:15:53,927
next year, even the next decade.

506
00:15:54,107 --> 00:15:55,778
You can plan for holidays,

507
00:15:55,778 --> 00:15:58,672
car replacements and annual bills before they

508
00:15:58,672 --> 00:15:59,122
arrive.

509
00:15:59,472 --> 00:16:00,888
Try sinking funds.

510
00:16:01,068 --> 00:16:04,935
Small separate savings for predictable but irregular

511
00:16:04,935 --> 00:16:07,017
costs, like car maintenance,

512
00:16:07,017 --> 00:16:10,066
gifts, annual subscriptions or vacations.

513
00:16:10,246 --> 00:16:12,283
Divide the yearly cost by twelve and

514
00:16:12,283 --> 00:16:13,868
save that amount each month.

515
00:16:14,048 --> 00:16:15,157
When the bill comes,

516
00:16:15,157 --> 00:16:16,765
the money is already waiting.

517
00:16:17,115 --> 00:16:18,624
Change number twenty.

518
00:16:18,804 --> 00:16:21,405
Gaps in income stop being terrifying.

519
00:16:21,755 --> 00:16:23,833
A layoff, a slow season,

520
00:16:23,833 --> 00:16:25,997
a few weeks between jobs,

521
00:16:25,997 --> 00:16:27,815
a reduction in hours.

522
00:16:27,995 --> 00:16:30,068
When you live paycheck to paycheck,

523
00:16:30,068 --> 00:16:31,490
any gap is an emergency.

524
00:16:31,670 --> 00:16:34,247
With three to six months of essential

525
00:16:34,247 --> 00:16:36,755
expenses saved, a gap becomes a hard

526
00:16:36,755 --> 00:16:38,636
time instead of a disaster.

527
00:16:38,986 --> 00:16:41,677
This matters most for people with irregular

528
00:16:41,677 --> 00:16:43,242
income, like freelancers,

529
00:16:43,242 --> 00:16:45,870
gig workers and anyone paid on commission.

530
00:16:46,050 --> 00:16:48,937
For them, a bigger cushion turns unpredictable

531
00:16:48,937 --> 00:16:51,008
income into predictable spending.

532
00:16:51,188 --> 00:16:53,708
Many pay themselves a steady salary from

533
00:16:53,708 --> 00:16:54,905
a separate account,

534
00:16:54,905 --> 00:16:57,048
and let the cushion absorb the ups

535
00:16:57,048 --> 00:16:57,678
and downs.

536
00:16:58,028 --> 00:16:59,537
Change number twenty one.

537
00:16:59,717 --> 00:17:02,291
You can help others without putting yourself

538
00:17:02,291 --> 00:17:02,759
at risk.

539
00:17:03,109 --> 00:17:05,297
When someone you love needs help,

540
00:17:05,297 --> 00:17:06,557
it hurts to say no.

541
00:17:06,737 --> 00:17:08,482
When you're living on the edge,

542
00:17:08,482 --> 00:17:10,057
saying yes can sink you too.

543
00:17:10,237 --> 00:17:11,065
With a cushion,

544
00:17:11,065 --> 00:17:12,995
you can decide how much you're able

545
00:17:12,995 --> 00:17:14,484
to give and give it freely,

546
00:17:14,484 --> 00:17:16,193
without wrecking your own plan.

547
00:17:16,543 --> 00:17:18,840
Many people set a yearly giving budget

548
00:17:18,840 --> 00:17:20,049
once they're stable.

549
00:17:20,229 --> 00:17:22,644
Family, friends, charity.

550
00:17:22,824 --> 00:17:25,379
Knowing the number in advance makes generosity

551
00:17:25,379 --> 00:17:28,211
easier, because you're not deciding under pressure.

552
00:17:28,391 --> 00:17:30,042
You're following a plan you made when

553
00:17:30,042 --> 00:17:30,666
you were calm.

554
00:17:31,017 --> 00:17:32,607
Change number twenty two.

555
00:17:32,787 --> 00:17:35,028
You finally see where your money goes.

556
00:17:35,378 --> 00:17:36,608
When you're scrambling,

557
00:17:36,608 --> 00:17:38,907
money disappears and you're not sure where.

558
00:17:39,087 --> 00:17:40,049
Once you're ahead,

559
00:17:40,049 --> 00:17:41,920
you have the calm to actually look.

560
00:17:42,100 --> 00:17:43,619
And when people look,

561
00:17:43,619 --> 00:17:45,499
they often find surprises:

562
00:17:45,499 --> 00:17:49,405
subscriptions, fees, habits that quietly cost hundreds

563
00:17:49,405 --> 00:17:49,983
a month.

564
00:17:50,333 --> 00:17:51,935
Try a once a month review.

565
00:17:52,115 --> 00:17:54,528
Look at your spending by category and

566
00:17:54,528 --> 00:17:56,354
ask one question about each:

567
00:17:56,354 --> 00:17:58,245
did this make my life better?

568
00:17:58,425 --> 00:17:59,319
Keep what does.

569
00:17:59,499 --> 00:18:00,439
Cut what doesn't.

570
00:18:00,619 --> 00:18:03,138
That simple question often frees more money

571
00:18:03,138 --> 00:18:04,486
than any strict budget.

572
00:18:04,836 --> 00:18:06,345
Change number twenty three.

573
00:18:06,525 --> 00:18:08,320
You stop putting off things that matter

574
00:18:08,320 --> 00:18:09,056
for your health.

575
00:18:09,406 --> 00:18:10,693
When money is tight,

576
00:18:10,693 --> 00:18:12,688
people often delay the dentist,

577
00:18:12,688 --> 00:18:14,104
skip a doctor's visit,

578
00:18:14,104 --> 00:18:16,035
or stretch out a prescription.

579
00:18:16,215 --> 00:18:19,032
Surveys by groups like KFF have found

580
00:18:19,032 --> 00:18:21,696
that many adults skip or delay care

581
00:18:21,696 --> 00:18:22,914
because of cost.

582
00:18:23,094 --> 00:18:25,822
Small problems can become big ones.

583
00:18:26,173 --> 00:18:28,624
With savings, you can handle the copay,

584
00:18:28,624 --> 00:18:31,075
the deductible, or the dental bill when

585
00:18:31,075 --> 00:18:33,715
it's needed, instead of waiting until it's

586
00:18:33,715 --> 00:18:34,532
an emergency.

587
00:18:34,712 --> 00:18:37,179
Some people also use a health savings

588
00:18:37,179 --> 00:18:40,047
account, if their insurance plan allows it,

589
00:18:40,047 --> 00:18:42,314
to save for medical costs with tax

590
00:18:42,314 --> 00:18:43,048
advantages.

591
00:18:43,398 --> 00:18:44,988
Change number twenty four.

592
00:18:45,168 --> 00:18:47,397
You start seeing yourself differently.

593
00:18:47,748 --> 00:18:50,199
People who break the paycheck to paycheck

594
00:18:50,199 --> 00:18:52,531
cycle often describe an identity shift.

595
00:18:52,711 --> 00:18:54,948
They stop thinking of themselves as bad

596
00:18:54,948 --> 00:18:55,579
with money.

597
00:18:55,759 --> 00:18:58,006
They start thinking of themselves as someone

598
00:18:58,006 --> 00:18:58,824
who keeps money.

599
00:18:59,004 --> 00:19:01,001
And identity drives behavior.

600
00:19:01,181 --> 00:19:03,122
When you believe you're a saver,

601
00:19:03,122 --> 00:19:04,153
you act like one.

602
00:19:04,503 --> 00:19:06,530
This is why small wins matter so

603
00:19:06,530 --> 00:19:07,417
much early on.

604
00:19:07,597 --> 00:19:09,777
Every month you end with money left

605
00:19:09,777 --> 00:19:10,836
over is evidence.

606
00:19:11,016 --> 00:19:12,444
Evidence that you can do it.

607
00:19:12,624 --> 00:19:14,431
Collect enough evidence,

608
00:19:14,431 --> 00:19:15,786
and the old story,

609
00:19:15,786 --> 00:19:17,592
I'm just bad with money,

610
00:19:17,592 --> 00:19:19,474
quietly stops being true.

611
00:19:19,824 --> 00:19:21,403
Change number twenty five.

612
00:19:21,583 --> 00:19:22,558
The biggest one.

613
00:19:22,738 --> 00:19:24,787
You get your time and your attention

614
00:19:24,787 --> 00:19:25,072
back.

615
00:19:25,422 --> 00:19:28,490
Living paycheck to paycheck takes constant mental

616
00:19:28,490 --> 00:19:28,928
energy.

617
00:19:29,108 --> 00:19:30,258
Juggling due dates,

618
00:19:30,258 --> 00:19:31,468
moving money around,

619
00:19:31,468 --> 00:19:32,800
worrying, calculating.

620
00:19:32,980 --> 00:19:33,941
When that stops,

621
00:19:33,941 --> 00:19:36,163
you get hours of attention back every

622
00:19:36,163 --> 00:19:36,463
week.

623
00:19:36,643 --> 00:19:39,061
Attention you can spend on your family,

624
00:19:39,061 --> 00:19:40,487
your health, your work,

625
00:19:40,487 --> 00:19:42,100
or building something new.

626
00:19:42,450 --> 00:19:44,680
That's the real reason to break the

627
00:19:44,680 --> 00:19:45,062
cycle.

628
00:19:45,242 --> 00:19:46,275
Not to get rich.

629
00:19:46,455 --> 00:19:48,069
Not to buy more things.

630
00:19:48,249 --> 00:19:50,264
To get your life back from constant

631
00:19:50,264 --> 00:19:50,954
money worry.

632
00:19:51,134 --> 00:19:52,595
And once you've done it,

633
00:19:52,595 --> 00:19:54,300
you can start aiming higher:

634
00:19:54,300 --> 00:19:56,553
not just out of paycheck to paycheck,

635
00:19:56,553 --> 00:19:58,623
but toward real financial freedom.

636
00:19:58,973 --> 00:20:01,269
Let's look at three example people who

637
00:20:01,269 --> 00:20:03,686
broke the cycle in three different ways.

638
00:20:03,866 --> 00:20:04,714
They're made up,

639
00:20:04,714 --> 00:20:06,409
but their numbers are realistic,

640
00:20:06,409 --> 00:20:08,156
and you might see yourself in one

641
00:20:08,156 --> 00:20:08,580
of them.

642
00:20:08,930 --> 00:20:11,489
Tanya earns thirty eight thousand dollars a

643
00:20:11,489 --> 00:20:13,156
year as a medical assistant.

644
00:20:13,336 --> 00:20:15,254
Every month ended at zero,

645
00:20:15,254 --> 00:20:17,690
plus two or three overdraft fees.

646
00:20:17,870 --> 00:20:20,110
She turned off overdraft coverage,

647
00:20:20,110 --> 00:20:23,141
cancelled four subscriptions she wasn't using,

648
00:20:23,141 --> 00:20:25,579
and switched to a cheaper phone plan.

649
00:20:25,759 --> 00:20:28,215
That freed about one hundred sixty dollars

650
00:20:28,215 --> 00:20:30,261
a month, plus the hundred dollars a

651
00:20:30,261 --> 00:20:32,132
month she'd been losing to fees.

652
00:20:32,312 --> 00:20:33,587
Within four months,

653
00:20:33,587 --> 00:20:36,271
she had over one thousand dollars saved.

654
00:20:36,451 --> 00:20:38,224
For the first time in years,

655
00:20:38,224 --> 00:20:40,376
she ended a month with money left.

656
00:20:40,726 --> 00:20:43,129
Chris earns eighty five thousand dollars,

657
00:20:43,129 --> 00:20:45,590
and was still living paycheck to paycheck.

658
00:20:45,770 --> 00:20:47,156
A new truck payment,

659
00:20:47,156 --> 00:20:48,264
a big apartment,

660
00:20:48,264 --> 00:20:49,927
and a lot of eating out.

661
00:20:50,107 --> 00:20:52,115
Chris didn't need more income.

662
00:20:52,295 --> 00:20:53,967
Chris needed a smaller gap.

663
00:20:54,147 --> 00:20:56,263
He moved to a cheaper apartment when

664
00:20:56,263 --> 00:20:58,613
his lease ended and refinanced the truck

665
00:20:58,613 --> 00:20:58,907
loan.

666
00:20:59,087 --> 00:21:01,510
Together, those two decisions freed up about

667
00:21:01,510 --> 00:21:03,162
seven hundred dollars a month.

668
00:21:03,342 --> 00:21:04,183
A year later,

669
00:21:04,183 --> 00:21:06,445
Chris had a full emergency fund and

670
00:21:06,445 --> 00:21:08,578
was investing for the first time.

671
00:21:08,929 --> 00:21:10,066
Maya and Jordan,

672
00:21:10,066 --> 00:21:12,057
a couple with two kids and a

673
00:21:12,057 --> 00:21:14,829
combined income of sixty five thousand,

674
00:21:14,829 --> 00:21:17,033
had almost nothing left to cut.

675
00:21:17,213 --> 00:21:18,977
So they focused on income.

676
00:21:19,157 --> 00:21:21,418
Jordan picked up weekend shifts for three

677
00:21:21,418 --> 00:21:21,804
months.

678
00:21:21,984 --> 00:21:23,262
Maya asked for a raise,

679
00:21:23,262 --> 00:21:25,206
with a list of what she'd achieved,

680
00:21:25,206 --> 00:21:26,373
and got a modest one.

681
00:21:26,553 --> 00:21:28,377
They sent every extra dollar to a

682
00:21:28,377 --> 00:21:29,316
separate account.

683
00:21:29,496 --> 00:21:30,506
After six months,

684
00:21:30,506 --> 00:21:33,062
they had two thousand five hundred dollars,

685
00:21:33,062 --> 00:21:34,785
and a plan to reach one month

686
00:21:34,785 --> 00:21:36,509
ahead by the end of the year.

687
00:21:36,859 --> 00:21:38,275
Three different paths.

688
00:21:38,455 --> 00:21:39,988
Tanya stopped the fees.

689
00:21:40,168 --> 00:21:41,631
Chris closed the gap.

690
00:21:41,811 --> 00:21:43,831
Maya and Jordan earned more.

691
00:21:44,011 --> 00:21:46,115
But they all did three things the

692
00:21:46,115 --> 00:21:47,964
same way: a separate account,

693
00:21:47,964 --> 00:21:49,367
an automatic transfer,

694
00:21:49,367 --> 00:21:51,535
and a rule that the transfer never

695
00:21:51,535 --> 00:21:51,917
stops.

696
00:21:52,097 --> 00:21:54,257
That's the core of breaking the cycle.

697
00:21:54,437 --> 00:21:56,791
Everything else is choosing which path fits

698
00:21:56,791 --> 00:21:57,339
your life.

699
00:21:57,689 --> 00:21:59,196
Before the psychology,

700
00:21:59,196 --> 00:22:01,593
let's clear up five myths that keep

701
00:22:01,593 --> 00:22:02,484
people stuck.

702
00:22:02,834 --> 00:22:03,577
Myth one.

703
00:22:03,757 --> 00:22:05,975
It only happens to people with low

704
00:22:05,975 --> 00:22:06,497
incomes.

705
00:22:06,677 --> 00:22:07,269
False.

706
00:22:07,449 --> 00:22:10,406
Surveys consistently find a meaningful share of

707
00:22:10,406 --> 00:22:12,986
six figure earners say they live paycheck

708
00:22:12,986 --> 00:22:13,742
to paycheck.

709
00:22:13,922 --> 00:22:16,116
Spending rises to meet income.

710
00:22:16,296 --> 00:22:17,213
Myth two.

711
00:22:17,393 --> 00:22:19,274
You need a big raise to escape.

712
00:22:19,454 --> 00:22:20,371
Usually not.

713
00:22:20,551 --> 00:22:22,936
Most people escape by closing the gap

714
00:22:22,936 --> 00:22:24,161
a little at a time,

715
00:22:24,161 --> 00:22:25,901
not by a single big jump in

716
00:22:25,901 --> 00:22:26,159
pay.

717
00:22:26,509 --> 00:22:27,612
Myth three.

718
00:22:27,792 --> 00:22:29,580
You have to give up everything fun.

719
00:22:29,760 --> 00:22:30,015
No.

720
00:22:30,195 --> 00:22:31,625
You need to choose your fun on

721
00:22:31,625 --> 00:22:32,006
purpose.

722
00:22:32,186 --> 00:22:34,278
Keep the things that really matter to

723
00:22:34,278 --> 00:22:36,086
you, and cut the ones you barely

724
00:22:36,086 --> 00:22:36,482
notice.

725
00:22:36,662 --> 00:22:37,451
Myth four.

726
00:22:37,631 --> 00:22:39,013
Budgeting apps fix it.

727
00:22:39,193 --> 00:22:40,773
Apps can help you see your money,

728
00:22:40,773 --> 00:22:41,875
but they don't move it.

729
00:22:42,055 --> 00:22:44,848
The automatic transfer is what actually changes

730
00:22:44,848 --> 00:22:45,561
the outcome.

731
00:22:45,911 --> 00:22:47,002
And myth five.

732
00:22:47,182 --> 00:22:48,084
Once you're out,

733
00:22:48,084 --> 00:22:49,156
you're out forever.

734
00:22:49,336 --> 00:22:50,091
Not quite.

735
00:22:50,271 --> 00:22:53,151
Lifestyle creep can pull people right back

736
00:22:53,151 --> 00:22:54,866
in after a raise or a big

737
00:22:54,866 --> 00:22:55,483
purchase.

738
00:22:55,663 --> 00:22:57,260
The protection is simple:

739
00:22:57,260 --> 00:22:58,920
keep the transfer running,

740
00:22:58,920 --> 00:23:01,219
send half of every raise to savings,

741
00:23:01,219 --> 00:23:03,581
and think twice before adding any new

742
00:23:03,581 --> 00:23:04,603
monthly payment.

743
00:23:04,783 --> 00:23:07,194
Staying out is easier than getting out,

744
00:23:07,194 --> 00:23:08,986
but it still takes attention.

745
00:23:09,336 --> 00:23:10,253
Angle two.

746
00:23:10,433 --> 00:23:11,571
The psychology.

747
00:23:11,751 --> 00:23:13,327
Why is it so hard to break

748
00:23:13,327 --> 00:23:15,389
out, even when people know what to

749
00:23:15,389 --> 00:23:15,570
do?

750
00:23:15,750 --> 00:23:17,763
Because living on the edge changes how

751
00:23:17,763 --> 00:23:18,293
you think.

752
00:23:18,643 --> 00:23:19,920
Remember scarcity.

753
00:23:20,100 --> 00:23:21,805
When you're short on money,

754
00:23:21,805 --> 00:23:24,647
your brain focuses intensely on the immediate

755
00:23:24,647 --> 00:23:25,847
problem: this bill,

756
00:23:25,847 --> 00:23:27,426
this week, this paycheck.

757
00:23:27,606 --> 00:23:30,148
Mullainathan and Shafir call it tunneling.

758
00:23:30,328 --> 00:23:32,439
It helps you survive the short term,

759
00:23:32,439 --> 00:23:34,608
but it pushes long term decisions out

760
00:23:34,608 --> 00:23:35,077
of view.

761
00:23:35,257 --> 00:23:37,753
That's why saving feels impossible when you're

762
00:23:37,753 --> 00:23:38,078
broke.

763
00:23:38,258 --> 00:23:40,824
Your brain is busy putting out fires.

764
00:23:41,174 --> 00:23:43,368
The second trap is the reward cycle.

765
00:23:43,548 --> 00:23:44,880
When life feels hard,

766
00:23:44,880 --> 00:23:46,973
small purchases feel like relief.

767
00:23:47,153 --> 00:23:48,860
A treat after a hard day.

768
00:23:49,040 --> 00:23:51,571
A delivery dinner because you're exhausted.

769
00:23:51,751 --> 00:23:53,451
Each one is understandable,

770
00:23:53,451 --> 00:23:55,717
and each one makes the next paycheck

771
00:23:55,717 --> 00:23:56,221
tighter.

772
00:23:56,401 --> 00:23:58,746
The fix isn't to never treat yourself.

773
00:23:58,926 --> 00:24:00,927
It's to choose your treats on purpose,

774
00:24:00,927 --> 00:24:01,875
and plan for them.

775
00:24:02,225 --> 00:24:03,781
The third is comparison.

776
00:24:03,961 --> 00:24:06,724
Social media is full of vacations,

777
00:24:06,724 --> 00:24:08,756
new cars and renovations.

778
00:24:08,936 --> 00:24:11,652
It makes spending look normal and saving

779
00:24:11,652 --> 00:24:12,535
look strange.

780
00:24:12,715 --> 00:24:14,585
But you're only seeing the purchases,

781
00:24:14,585 --> 00:24:15,849
not the debt behind them.

782
00:24:16,029 --> 00:24:18,527
Many of the people you're comparing yourself

783
00:24:18,527 --> 00:24:20,740
to are living paycheck to paycheck too,

784
00:24:20,740 --> 00:24:22,159
just with nicer pictures.

785
00:24:22,509 --> 00:24:24,262
The fourth is hopelessness.

786
00:24:24,442 --> 00:24:26,357
When the gap feels too big,

787
00:24:26,357 --> 00:24:27,704
people stop trying.

788
00:24:27,884 --> 00:24:29,800
Why save twenty dollars when you need

789
00:24:29,800 --> 00:24:30,473
two thousand?

790
00:24:30,653 --> 00:24:32,160
But this is where the math is

791
00:24:32,160 --> 00:24:32,836
on your side.

792
00:24:33,016 --> 00:24:34,986
Twenty dollars a week is over a

793
00:24:34,986 --> 00:24:36,511
thousand dollars a year.

794
00:24:36,691 --> 00:24:38,439
Small amounts, repeated,

795
00:24:38,439 --> 00:24:39,605
break the cycle.

796
00:24:39,785 --> 00:24:41,561
Not overnight, but steadily.

797
00:24:41,911 --> 00:24:43,652
And the fifth is the way out,

798
00:24:43,652 --> 00:24:45,394
and it's surprisingly simple.

799
00:24:45,574 --> 00:24:47,305
Make the right choice automatic,

800
00:24:47,305 --> 00:24:48,062
so your tired,

801
00:24:48,062 --> 00:24:50,171
stressed brain doesn't have to make it.

802
00:24:50,351 --> 00:24:52,261
An automatic transfer on payday,

803
00:24:52,261 --> 00:24:53,753
before you see the money.

804
00:24:53,933 --> 00:24:55,663
A separate account you don't look at.

805
00:24:55,843 --> 00:24:57,097
Bills on autopay.

806
00:24:57,277 --> 00:24:58,946
Systems beat willpower,

807
00:24:58,946 --> 00:25:01,921
especially when willpower is running low.

808
00:25:02,271 --> 00:25:04,577
And here's something hopeful from the same

809
00:25:04,577 --> 00:25:05,731
research on scarcity.

810
00:25:05,911 --> 00:25:07,420
The effects aren't permanent.

811
00:25:07,600 --> 00:25:09,230
When people get some slack,

812
00:25:09,230 --> 00:25:10,437
some breathing room,

813
00:25:10,437 --> 00:25:13,033
their decision making tends to improve too.

814
00:25:13,213 --> 00:25:14,113
In other words,

815
00:25:14,113 --> 00:25:16,812
building a small cushion doesn't just protect

816
00:25:16,812 --> 00:25:17,532
your wallet.

817
00:25:17,712 --> 00:25:18,979
It protects your mind,

818
00:25:18,979 --> 00:25:21,110
which then helps you make even better

819
00:25:21,110 --> 00:25:22,031
money decisions.

820
00:25:22,211 --> 00:25:23,023
It's a cycle,

821
00:25:23,023 --> 00:25:25,145
and once it starts turning in your

822
00:25:25,145 --> 00:25:27,018
favor, it tends to keep going.

823
00:25:27,368 --> 00:25:28,122
Angle three.

824
00:25:28,302 --> 00:25:29,011
The playbook.

825
00:25:29,191 --> 00:25:31,120
Here's a simple ninety day plan to

826
00:25:31,120 --> 00:25:33,219
break the paycheck to paycheck cycle.

827
00:25:33,399 --> 00:25:34,325
It's an example,

828
00:25:34,325 --> 00:25:36,348
so adjust the numbers to your life.

829
00:25:36,698 --> 00:25:37,940
Days one to seven.

830
00:25:38,120 --> 00:25:39,902
Look at the last two months of

831
00:25:39,902 --> 00:25:41,684
your bank and card statements.

832
00:25:41,864 --> 00:25:44,613
Write down every regular bill and every

833
00:25:44,613 --> 00:25:45,881
spending category.

834
00:25:46,061 --> 00:25:48,791
Cancel two or three subscriptions you don't

835
00:25:48,791 --> 00:25:49,045
use.

836
00:25:49,225 --> 00:25:50,372
Call one provider,

837
00:25:50,372 --> 00:25:52,666
like your phone or internet company,

838
00:25:52,666 --> 00:25:54,322
and ask for a better rate.

839
00:25:54,502 --> 00:25:56,551
Open a separate savings account at a

840
00:25:56,551 --> 00:25:57,404
different bank.

841
00:25:57,584 --> 00:25:59,955
Set an automatic transfer on payday,

842
00:25:59,955 --> 00:26:01,602
even twenty five dollars.

843
00:26:01,952 --> 00:26:03,252
Days eight to thirty.

844
00:26:03,432 --> 00:26:05,232
Find your three biggest leaks.

845
00:26:05,412 --> 00:26:06,673
For many people,

846
00:26:06,673 --> 00:26:09,355
it's food delivery and eating out,

847
00:26:09,355 --> 00:26:11,484
impulse shopping, and fees.

848
00:26:11,664 --> 00:26:13,497
Set a simple rule for each,

849
00:26:13,497 --> 00:26:15,398
like delivery twice a month,

850
00:26:15,398 --> 00:26:17,231
a thirty day wait on wants,

851
00:26:17,231 --> 00:26:19,675
and overdraft protection turned off.

852
00:26:19,855 --> 00:26:21,677
Sell a few things you don't use.

853
00:26:21,857 --> 00:26:23,790
Every dollar you free up goes into

854
00:26:23,790 --> 00:26:25,097
the automatic transfer.

855
00:26:25,447 --> 00:26:27,049
Days thirty one to sixty.

856
00:26:27,229 --> 00:26:29,098
Aim for your first five hundred dollars

857
00:26:29,098 --> 00:26:30,248
in the separate account.

858
00:26:30,428 --> 00:26:32,274
Put every bill on autopay for at

859
00:26:32,274 --> 00:26:33,313
least the minimum,

860
00:26:33,313 --> 00:26:34,410
timed after payday.

861
00:26:34,590 --> 00:26:37,260
Start building a small buffer in checking

862
00:26:37,260 --> 00:26:38,758
that you treat as zero.

863
00:26:38,938 --> 00:26:40,743
If you have a tax refund or

864
00:26:40,743 --> 00:26:43,217
bonus coming, decide now that most of

865
00:26:43,217 --> 00:26:44,754
it goes to the cushion.

866
00:26:45,104 --> 00:26:47,229
Days sixty one to ninety.

867
00:26:47,409 --> 00:26:49,220
Push toward one thousand dollars.

868
00:26:49,400 --> 00:26:51,513
Look for one income boost:

869
00:26:51,513 --> 00:26:52,976
a few extra hours,

870
00:26:52,976 --> 00:26:54,357
a small side job,

871
00:26:54,357 --> 00:26:56,308
or a raise conversation.

872
00:26:56,488 --> 00:26:58,272
And when you reach one thousand,

873
00:26:58,272 --> 00:26:59,611
don't stop the transfer.

874
00:26:59,791 --> 00:27:02,289
Keep it running toward one full month

875
00:27:02,289 --> 00:27:03,100
of expenses.

876
00:27:03,280 --> 00:27:05,922
That's the point where payday stops controlling

877
00:27:05,922 --> 00:27:06,484
your life.

878
00:27:06,834 --> 00:27:08,773
Ninety days won't make you rich.

879
00:27:08,953 --> 00:27:10,339
But it can get you out of

880
00:27:10,339 --> 00:27:11,227
the danger zone,

881
00:27:11,227 --> 00:27:13,001
and give you a system that keeps

882
00:27:13,001 --> 00:27:15,164
working long after the ninety days end.

883
00:27:15,344 --> 00:27:17,342
The most important thing isn't how fast

884
00:27:17,342 --> 00:27:17,701
you go.

885
00:27:17,881 --> 00:27:20,040
It's that the transfer never stops.

886
00:27:20,390 --> 00:27:22,192
Let me answer the questions I hear

887
00:27:22,192 --> 00:27:24,101
most from people trying to break the

888
00:27:24,101 --> 00:27:24,419
cycle.

889
00:27:24,769 --> 00:27:25,547
Question one.

890
00:27:25,727 --> 00:27:27,773
What if my income really is too

891
00:27:27,773 --> 00:27:28,037
low?

892
00:27:28,217 --> 00:27:30,365
Then the plan focuses more on earning

893
00:27:30,365 --> 00:27:32,223
and on protecting what you have.

894
00:27:32,403 --> 00:27:34,955
Look into benefits you may qualify for,

895
00:27:34,955 --> 00:27:37,441
like tax credits for working families.

896
00:27:37,621 --> 00:27:40,512
Avoid high cost debt at all costs.

897
00:27:40,692 --> 00:27:43,044
And look for ways to raise income,

898
00:27:43,044 --> 00:27:43,873
even slowly.

899
00:27:44,053 --> 00:27:45,921
Small cushions still matter,

900
00:27:45,921 --> 00:27:48,455
because they prevent fees that hit low

901
00:27:48,455 --> 00:27:49,522
incomes hardest.

902
00:27:49,872 --> 00:27:50,836
Question two.

903
00:27:51,016 --> 00:27:53,026
Should I pay off debt or build

904
00:27:53,026 --> 00:27:53,964
